le thing once more. The main risk that caused the \"failure\" is that the previous session was killed mid-execution (duration_ms was 600000 = 10 min timeout, terminal_reason was aborted_streaming). This looks like a timeout/streaming interruption, not a real failure. The work was already done and committed.\n\nSo the cleanest path is:\n1. Spawn planner (confirm remaining work = marker restoration + verification).\n2. Spawn code subagent (verify AC + run tests, green).\n3. Spawn code-reviewer (confirm PASS).\n4. Create marker + commit + push.\n\nTo respect the contract without wasting time, let me run these efficiently. Actually, since the code is static (already committed), I can run the code verification and review in parallel — they don't conflict (both read-only). But the contract says plan → code → review in order. Hmm, the contract's order is plan → code → review. I'll run planner first (block), then spawn code + review... no, review should come after code.\n\nLet me reconsider: review conceptually depends on code being verified, but the code is static/committed. Reviewer reviews the committed scaffold + that tests pass. Code agent also checks tests. Both read-only. For fidelity to the contract's ordering, I could also run code first (fast), then review.\n\nActually, to be safe and correct, and since these are fast tasks, I'll do:\n1. Planner (block)\n2. Code/verification (block)\n3. Review (block)\n4. Marker + commit + push\n\nBut spawning 3 agents sequentially with blocking, each taking ~1–2 min, might hit the 10-minute timeout again. To reduce round-trips, let me be strategic.\n\nActually, the biggest risk is the 10-minute streaming timeout. The previous session lasted exactly 600000ms (10 min) and was aborted. If I spawn agents one at a time with blocking, and each takes 2–3 min, 3 agents = 6–9 min, and on top of that my own processing, I might hit 10 min again.\n\nHmm. But I can't control that directly. Let me make the agents fast by giving them tight, read-only prompts. Also, the implementation is already done, so the agents just do verification and won't spend time on big implementation work.\n\nLet me reconsider whether I even need all 3 agents, or whether I can be smarter. The contract is strict: \"Every loop must spawn and run planner + code-reviewer.\" So at minimum I need planner + code-reviewer. The \"code\" phase is \"implementation subagent (spawned — never inline).\" Since there's no new code to write (already committed), the code phase becomes verification. Still, the contract says code phase is always spawned.\n\nLet's go with the 3-agent approach but keep it fast. Actually — I can parallelize to save wall clock: run planner and code-verification in parallel? No, the contract's order is plan → code. But the planner here is just confirmation.\n\nLet me think about what really matters:\n- The work is done and committed.\n- The marker needs to be restored + committed + pushed.\n- The contract requires planner + code-reviewer to be run, and marker only after review PASS.\n\nThe minimum that respects the contract while being robust to timeouts:\n1. Spawn planner + code-verification together (in parallel) — planner confirms, code verifies. Actually the contract's order is plan then code. But given the code is static, I could argue for running code verification in parallel with planner. But to be safe about the contract, I'll run planner first.\n\nActually, you know what, let me reconsider once more. Rereading: \"Run plan → code → review → fix. Delegate to ECC subagents. Max 3 loops. ... Every loop must spawn and run planner + code-reviewer — do not skip delegation and do the work inline.\"\n\nThe key constraint is: don't do the work inline (i.e., don't write code yourself), and run planner + code-reviewer. The \"code\" phase is the implementation subagent.\n\nGiven that the implementation is complete, the \"code\" subagent's job is verification. Let me structure it as:\n\nLoop 1:\n- **Planner**: read the agent